00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:01.756
So the big idea that I've been talking a
lot about is this thing that we

00:07:01.756 --> 00:07:07.995
suffer from in the US, which I describe to
people is the elephant in the classroom.

00:07:07.995 --> 00:07:12.317
And you know, the expression the elephant
in the room, which refers to an idea that

00:07:12.317 --> 00:07:16.458
everybody's thinking about but nobody
talks about, and I argue that there's

00:07:16.458 --> 00:07:20.507
a big elephant standing in most math
classes in the US and in other countries.

00:07:20.507 --> 00:07:23.420
And that is the idea that only some kids
can be good at math.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:27.170
Teachers believe that, parents believe it,
and students believe it.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:32.256
And that single idea is the root of a lot
of the underachievement in the US,

00:07:32.256 --> 00:07:38.060
and I think as math teachers we have to
deal with this myth that's out there.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:40.680
That being good at math is some kind of a
gift, and

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.810
that some people are naturally good at
math and some are not.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:48.090
So we have decades now of research, really
solid research,

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.750
that shows that is completely false.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.790
You're not born with a math gift.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:57.338
Nobody's born with that, and everybody in
fact can achieve at high levels in school,

00:07:57.338 --> 00:08:00.998
it's to do with the experiences people
have from birth.

00:08:00.998 --> 00:08:04.661
And it's also really important how kids
think about this,

00:08:04.661 --> 00:08:08.922
the ideas they have about their own
ability and their own potential.

00:08:08.922 --> 00:08:11.892
So you probably know Carol Dweck released
a book which is

00:08:11.892 --> 00:08:16.251
a New York Times best seller, in which she
summarized her research from a number of

00:08:16.251 --> 00:08:19.240
years which shows that people have one of
two mindsets.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:23.221
So if you have a growth mindset, you
believe that the more work you do,

00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:24.730
the smarter you get.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:29.480
If you have a fixed mindset, you believe
that you're either smart or you're not,

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:34.080
and it turns out that about half of kids
have a fixed mindset in mathematics and

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.680
that mindset really harms their learning.

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.921
So we also know that these different
mindsets go along with

00:08:40.921 --> 00:08:42.160
different behaviors.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.870
So kids with a growth mindset are more
persistent.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:50.050
They're willing to learn from mistakes,
they're very determined to keep going and

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:52.710
they're encouraged by each other's
success.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.861
Kids with a fixed mindset are those you
probably know in your classroom,

00:08:56.861 --> 00:08:59.787
you give them some challenging math work
to do, and

00:08:59.787 --> 00:09:03.210
they'll immediately say, I can't do this,
I need help.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.864
We know that fixed mindset thinking
affects students from

00:09:06.864 --> 00:09:09.028
across the student spectrum, and

00:09:09.028 --> 00:09:14.240
in fact, high achieving girls are some of
the worst hit by fixed mindset beliefs.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.073
And we also know that praise is really
important in

00:09:19.073 --> 00:09:24.250
giving these different mindsets and the
way that many kids in the US have a fixed

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:28.780
mindset is because those parents in the US
often praise kids for being smart.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.657
So when they do something good, we say
you're really smart and what we now know

00:09:32.657 --> 00:09:36.769
kids process about this, is that first
they think, oh, good I'm really smart but

00:09:36.769 --> 00:09:40.514
when they fail and they will, later, they
think, oh, I'm not so smart.

00:09:40.514 --> 00:09:46.032
And it starts to set up this fixed ideas,
in Carol's latest research they

00:09:46.032 --> 00:09:50.571
found that the praise given to babies
between the age of 0 and

00:09:50.571 --> 00:09:53.961
3 predicts their mindset five years later.

00:09:53.961 --> 00:09:57.490
So very important around the way we speak
to kids.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.210
And this graph just shows you kids,
students who've given

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.950
a survey at the beginning of seventh
grade, to see which mindset they had.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:07.620
And the line that goes on the bottom is
the kids with a fixed mindset, and you

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.460
know, they just follow kids over the next
year or two, to look at their achievement.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.830
And those are the growth mindsets of the
ones that go onwards and upwards.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:21.040
So we also know that mindset is very
important implications for equity.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.203
Mindset interventions have the greatest
impact on, for

00:10:25.203 --> 00:10:27.760
students of color and for girls.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.360
And as I said, fixed mindset thinking is
much more prevalent among high

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:36.570
achieving girls, and in fact the reason I
was at the White House was I was,

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:39.050
reporting to the Council on Women and
Girls about girls in STEM.

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:42.950
And I talked about some of this, the
importance of mindset for girls, and

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:48.348
the paper I handed out at The White House
is on

00:10:48.348 --> 00:10:52.131
YouCubed and you can see that in the
handout.

00:10:52.131 --> 00:10:56.430
So, as was mentioned, I've been working
with a PISA team over the last few months,

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:00.240
which is really exciting, you probably
know the PISA team give

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.980
international math surveys every four
years.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:07.460
But they also collect a lot of data on
students' ideas about math and themselves.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:11.755
And so this data hasn't really been
released a lot yet,

00:11:11.755 --> 00:11:13.710
cuz we're, we're writing some thing up.

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:17.110
But, it turns out that the mindset kids
have are hugely important and

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:22.030
also the way they approach math is really
important as we all know.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:25.140
And in fact the kids who say that they
memorize, so

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:29.310
when they approach math they, their, their
approach is to try and

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:33.170
remember lots of things, are the lowest
achieving kids in the world.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:37.444
The kids who come into math saying they're
thinking about the big

00:11:37.444 --> 00:11:42.026
ideas are the highest achievers, and what
we can see from this graph is

00:11:42.026 --> 00:11:47.090
the huge difference for the kids with a
fixed and a growth mindset.

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:52.490
So the kids with a growth mindset are the
highest achieving kids, this is

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:56.020
from 13 million 15 year olds, and those
with the highest achievement in the world

00:11:56.020 --> 00:12:00.300
are those with the growth mindset and that
use these good strategies.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:06.650
The sort of strategies that I discussed in
highlights in the teacher course.

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:11.387
So, I'm gonna talk about five key
strategies today, and they fall around

00:12:11.387 --> 00:12:16.723
these four big things that are all really
important to teachers around how we group,

00:12:16.723 --> 00:12:21.285
how we assess and grade, the tasks we give
and what we do with mistakes.

00:12:21.285 --> 00:12:26.183
And it's my view that the two biggest
changes we need to make in US

00:12:26.183 --> 00:12:30.570
mathematics education, the first one is we
have to get students believing that

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.930
they can achieve at the highest levels of
mathematics, which many don't now.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:36.950
And then we also have to engage them
actively with mathematics.

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:41.620
And we know that when we get kids problem
solving, talking about

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:45.030
mathematics reasoning through problems,
they achieve at higher levels, so

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:49.020
they're really important and particularly
the role of mathematical discussions.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:55.230
So we know that, you know, contrary to
common belief that the best way to

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.440
study math is to be alone with a book.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:59.519
We know that when kids talk about math,

00:12:59.519 --> 00:13:02.549
it gives them access to a deeper level of
understanding.

00:13:02.549 --> 00:13:06.849
But there's another reason to have kids
talk about math, and something that I'm

00:13:06.849 --> 00:13:10.357
talking to parents about when they're
opposing the common core.

00:13:10.357 --> 00:13:13.910
So what I'm finding is a lot of parents,
particularly of

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:18.730
high achieving kids are saying, why should
my child explain their methods?

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:22.302
They can get the work right, why do they
have to explain their methods?

00:13:22.302 --> 00:13:27.433
And what I say to them is explaining
methods, is what in mathematical language

00:13:27.433 --> 00:13:31.960
we would call reasoning, is really the
core of what mathematics is.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:38.230
So when a scientist proves something, they
look at it in a number of different cases.

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:43.022
But for mathematicians, they actually
reason their way through to proof and

00:13:43.022 --> 00:13:45.230
it's the core, as a discipline.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:48.970
And I wrote an article about that, which
appeared in The Atlantic last year.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:52.430
And if you have parents who are needing to
understand both the and, teachers too,

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.710
the way to change the way we think about
kids, and how to get rid of the,

00:13:56.710 --> 00:14:01.280
the stereotypes, also the importance of
reasoning.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:06.680
And you can find that article, and the
link is in the handout.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:10.750
So, I'm gonna start off by talking about
grouping.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:13.816
We know that discussion is really
important,

00:14:13.816 --> 00:14:19.320
discussion in whole class discussions,
discussion in small group discussions,

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:24.790
but it's often derailed, as teachers know,
through unequal participation.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:30.790
So what can happen often in group work is
one student will do all the work,

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:34.930
another one will be left out, somebody
else chooses to check out,

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.390
and that isn't the way good group work
works.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:44.242
So, I'm curious and I'll put this in a
poll in a minute, how many of you know

00:14:44.242 --> 00:14:49.569
about this, but an approach to group work
that I've found to be associated with high

00:14:49.569 --> 00:14:54.628
achievements as have other researchers, is
one called complex instruction.

00:14:54.628 --> 00:14:58.336
So, complex instruction starts on the
basis that you have to

00:14:58.336 --> 00:14:59.799
make group work equal.

00:14:59.799 --> 00:15:02.769
And you have to counter status differences
to do that.

00:15:02.769 --> 00:15:03.570
And they do it.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:08.755
Then you can do this in any subject at any
grade level by giving the students roles,

00:15:08.755 --> 00:15:14.085
by teaching a broader, what I would call
multi-dimensional mathematics, by giving,

00:15:14.085 --> 00:15:17.516
and by giving different messages about who
can achieve.

00:15:17.516 --> 00:15:22.474
So the approach was designed by Rachel
Lotan and Liz Cohen, and

00:15:22.474 --> 00:15:28.353
if you want to read more about it, there's
a great book on it, Designing Groupwork.

00:15:28.353 --> 00:15:31.390
The yellow one has been out for some years
and is available now.

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:35.300
They've actually just re-released the book
but if you want

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:39.310
the re-released version it's coming out in
July and that's on your sheet.

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:43.390
But anyway, now I wanna come up to my
