00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:10.878
But there's something particular they do
inside complex instruction, and

00:17:10.878 --> 00:17:12.180
I'm gonna teach you.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.210
Before I do that some other things we

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:18.280
talked about in the course was the way we
assess and grade kids.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:23.209
Obviously, the way kids get feedback on
their work hugely impacts their mindset.

00:17:23.209 --> 00:17:28.604
And one of the things we talk about in the
online course is how replacing grades

00:17:28.604 --> 00:17:34.674
with diagnostic comments is really a
positive thing for students' engagement.

00:17:34.674 --> 00:17:38.025
So, we tend to grade a lot in the US.

00:17:38.025 --> 00:17:41.209
In other countries, kids get the grade at
the end of the year.

00:17:41.209 --> 00:17:45.535
And what we know from studies is that when
teachers stop grading all together and

00:17:45.535 --> 00:17:47.020
give diagnostic comment,

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:51.235
telling kids what they need to do to
improve achievement really goes up.

00:17:51.235 --> 00:17:55.558
So, but I know as well and I, you know,
from all the work I do with teachers,

00:17:55.558 --> 00:17:59.493
that particularly teachers in the US feel
that their hands are tied.

00:17:59.493 --> 00:18:02.743
And that they have to be grading a lot
because the school

00:18:02.743 --> 00:18:07.100
administration requires it, the parents
require it, districts require it.

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:12.343
So, one of the things that I recommend for
them is to switch the grading.

00:18:12.343 --> 00:18:16.498
So, instead of grading always being on,
sort of of how many answers are correct,

00:18:16.498 --> 00:18:20.670
actually grade on the participation you
want to see in the classroom.

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:26.167
And once you start grading on that, kids
participation drastically improves.

00:18:26.167 --> 00:18:29.849
So, this leads me to my first strategy,
which I'm really loving at the moment.

00:18:29.849 --> 00:18:32.984
So, this is something called a
participation quiz, and

00:18:32.984 --> 00:18:36.658
those of you who know complex instruction
may know this strategy.

00:18:36.658 --> 00:18:41.635
So, I taught this to the teachers I work
within Vista school district, and

00:18:41.635 --> 00:18:45.590
they did the participation quiz the first
day with a group.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:48.206
And they tell me by the time the groups
met for

00:18:48.206 --> 00:18:52.115
the second time, it has transformed the
way kids work in groups.

00:18:52.115 --> 00:18:56.222
So, the way it works is, students work in
a group on some math work, and

00:18:56.222 --> 00:19:00.603
they are literally graded, the group is
graded, at the end of the lesson for

00:19:00.603 --> 00:19:02.948
their participation in the group work.

00:19:02.948 --> 00:19:06.511
So, the teachers are actually looking for
how they participate.

00:19:06.511 --> 00:19:08.404
Are they leaning in and talking to each
other?

00:19:08.404 --> 00:19:11.113
Are they asking good questions?

00:19:11.113 --> 00:19:14.223
Are they working equally?

00:19:14.223 --> 00:19:18.479
So, I'm gonna take you through how to run
a participation quiz.

00:19:18.479 --> 00:19:21.746
It would be much better if we could watch
videos and talk about this in a PD

00:19:21.746 --> 00:19:25.460
session, but I'm hoping this will help to
just try it once and see what happens.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:28.560
So, this is how you run one.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:33.810
You just choose an ordinary day of group
work, where kids are working on math

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:35.880
tasks together, and then you tell kids
they're gonna get a grade for

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.260
how they work in the group at the end of
the lesson.

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:42.030
And then, it's very important, you show
students what you're looking for

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.083
in their group work.

00:19:43.083 --> 00:19:47.026
So, the example I'm gonna show you, the
teachers share with students.

00:19:47.026 --> 00:19:50.316
We're looking for these kind of
interactions, and

00:19:50.316 --> 00:19:53.614
we're looking for this kind of
mathematical work.

00:19:53.614 --> 00:19:58.706
Now, then you either get a piece of paper
or some teachers do it on the board

00:19:58.706 --> 00:20:04.307
publicly, on, on a, whatever form of
display, into sections for each group.

00:20:04.307 --> 00:20:07.991
So, if you have 32 students, and working
groups of four,

00:20:07.991 --> 00:20:12.800
which complex instruction recommends, you
might have eight sections.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:17.792
And as students are working on their
groups, teachers circulate the room,

00:20:17.792 --> 00:20:22.160
just writing down comments inside the, the
boxes for each group.

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:26.153
How well they're working, any good
questions that come up, and

00:20:26.153 --> 00:20:29.942
then at the end of the lesson, they assign
each group a grade.

00:20:29.942 --> 00:20:35.503
So the teachers I work with, I'm just
pulling their slides that they've used for

00:20:35.503 --> 00:20:38.027
a complex, for a participation quiz.

00:20:38.027 --> 00:20:42.692
Just to give you an idea, but they show to
the students before these.

00:20:42.692 --> 00:20:44.397
So, this is the mathematical slide.

00:20:44.397 --> 00:20:45.285
There's a lot on here.

00:20:45.285 --> 00:20:47.722
I'm not sure I would use as many,
particularly for

00:20:47.722 --> 00:20:50.225
younger kids you might wanna highlight two
things.

00:20:50.225 --> 00:20:53.767
So, this shows you a full range of things,
the sort of things you could put up here.

00:20:53.767 --> 00:20:58.078
But, so they say we want you to be
recognizing and describing patterns.

00:20:58.078 --> 00:21:01.472
Justifying thinking using multiple
representations.

00:21:01.472 --> 00:21:05.926
Making connections between different
approaches and representations.

00:21:05.926 --> 00:21:10.871
Using words, arrows, numbers, and color
coding to communicate ideas clearly,

00:21:10.871 --> 00:21:14.193
explain ideas clearly to team members and
the teacher.

00:21:14.193 --> 00:21:18.080
Ask questions to understand the thinking
of other team members,

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:22.744
ask questions that push the group to go
deeper, and organize a presentation so

00:21:22.744 --> 00:21:26.970
the people outside the group can
understand your group's thinking.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:32.040
And, sort of, the mantra of complex
instruction that the teachers use

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:34.110
with it is this message to kids.

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:37.360
No one is good at all of these things, but
everyone is good at something.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:41.311
And you need all of your group members to
be successful.

00:21:41.311 --> 00:21:45.298
So, these are the kind of mathematical
things they're looking for and

00:21:45.298 --> 00:21:48.697
then the teachers had very concrete things
group behavior.

00:21:48.697 --> 00:21:51.814
We're looking for you to be leaning in and
working the middle of the table.

00:21:51.814 --> 00:21:55.425
So, a nice strategy for group work is a
sort of tip really is

00:21:55.425 --> 00:21:59.719
to only have one work piece of paper, cuz
if kids have their own paper,

00:21:59.719 --> 00:22:03.010
they are very likely to pull off to the
side and work on it.

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:06.206
So, one piece of, one task paper, are they
leaning in and

00:22:06.206 --> 00:22:11.200
working in the middle of the table, is it
equal air time are they sticking together,

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:15.928
are they listening to each other, are they
asking each other lots of questions, and

00:22:15.928 --> 00:22:17.750
then following the team roles?

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:21.461
So, the teacher, I might have a sheet,
something like this, and walk around

00:22:21.461 --> 00:22:25.246
making notes on a clipboard or as I said,
I might have it at the front of the room.

00:22:25.246 --> 00:22:29.478
And I'm just gonna share with you an
example of one teacher's notes at

00:22:29.478 --> 00:22:31.496
the end of a participation quiz.

00:22:31.496 --> 00:22:35.564
So, you can see they're both, they've
graded the groups but

00:22:35.564 --> 00:22:41.245
also at some points they've just said
great discussion, working well together,

00:22:41.245 --> 00:22:43.492
building on each other's ideas.

00:22:43.492 --> 00:22:47.051
Sometimes there's a question written down
that they, is a quote from the kids.

00:22:47.051 --> 00:22:51.401
Can anyone see a different way, you can
see the see the bottom left hand

00:22:51.401 --> 00:22:56.440
group weren't working so well together
talking about clothes, off task.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:23:01.239
So, what happened with the teachers that I
work with in Vista school districts is

00:23:01.239 --> 00:23:03.922
they tried a participation quiz one day,
and

00:23:03.922 --> 00:23:08.670
they told the, the tea, the kids, we're
gonna be grading you in a group work.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:10.251
They went down making notes.

00:23:10.251 --> 00:23:14.256
And I guess the, the kids didn't really
pay much pay much attention to that.

00:23:14.256 --> 00:23:17.522
And at the end of the day, they gave the
grades and talked about it.

00:23:17.522 --> 00:23:20.738
So, what they said was the next time they
did group work,

00:23:20.738 --> 00:23:25.518
the very next time, the kids' interactions
in the groups had massively improved.

00:23:25.518 --> 00:23:30.197
So, they're really loving using this as a
group work strategy.

00:23:30.197 --> 00:23:35.374
So, this a quick overview, but I'm curious
and I put in another poll here to see.

00:23:35.374 --> 00:23:38.173
My question is, do you think you would try
