00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:52.730
So my next thing I want to talk about a
little bit is classroom math tasks.

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:57.390
So, in the teacher online course, we spend
quite a bit of time on tasks and

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:02.390
thinking about what makes a really good
task for students.

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:06.400
I argue that it's very hard to have a
growth mindset in math when all you work

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:10.510
on in math class is a series of short
closed question that you

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:12.330
really get right or wrong.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:16.150
So, those kinda question don't convey to
kids that math is about learning.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:21.370
And in fact, we know that a lot of
students actually don't think that

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:24.252
they're in math class to learn.

00:25:24.252 --> 00:25:25.874
They think that they're there to perform.

00:25:25.874 --> 00:25:28.435
And they'll tell you that when they go
into math class,

00:25:28.435 --> 00:25:30.570
what's important is to get questions
right.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:34.162
So, they're not so oriented around
learning as much as performance.

00:25:34.162 --> 00:25:38.939
And this really came home to me recently
when a colleague of mine, Rachel Lambert,

00:25:38.939 --> 00:25:43.729
told me her six-year old son came home one
day, and said he hadn't liked math class.

00:25:43.729 --> 00:25:45.750
And she said, why, you know, what
happened?

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:51.101
And he said, math is too much answer time
and not enough learning time.

00:25:51.101 --> 00:25:54.253
So, that's a really perceptive comment
from a six year old, but

00:25:54.253 --> 00:25:57.020
it, you know, you can see how kids get
this impression.

00:25:57.020 --> 00:25:59.860
That they go into math class and they're
answering questions.

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:05.869
So, what I do in my work with teachers,
and we talk about the teaching course is,

00:26:05.869 --> 00:26:11.256
tasks need to give students the space
inside them to learn something.

00:26:11.256 --> 00:26:12.039
If a task really,

00:26:12.039 --> 00:26:15.501
is just answering a question, that doesn't
really have space for learning.

00:26:15.501 --> 00:26:20.709
So, I shared this framework we've
developed for the Growth Mindset Tasks.

00:26:20.709 --> 00:26:22.769
They need to focus on learning and

00:26:22.769 --> 00:26:26.160
have opportunities to learn something
inside them.

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:30.450
So to do this, the main thing is to open
up Math Tasks.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:32.922
And you can open them in different ways.

00:26:32.922 --> 00:26:37.039
You can have a task where you ask kids
this, how they see it, and

00:26:37.039 --> 00:26:39.545
I'll show you an example in a minute.

00:26:39.545 --> 00:26:44.030
You can have tasks with multiple entry
points, tasks that value different

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:49.398
pathways and strategies, tasks that focus
on sense making rather then an answer.

00:26:49.398 --> 00:26:53.328
And I'll, we'll look at a little example.

00:26:53.328 --> 00:26:59.135
So this example comes from a wonderful
teacher, Kathy Humphries, and

00:26:59.135 --> 00:27:05.735
it's actually in a book of video cases of
her teaching her 7th grade class.

00:27:05.735 --> 00:27:09.927
So, it's called, the book's called
Connecting Mathematical Ideas, but

00:27:09.927 --> 00:27:11.589
one divided by two-thirds is,

00:27:11.589 --> 00:27:15.793
could totally be a closed one answer
questions that students just answer.

00:27:15.793 --> 00:27:19.515
But she opens this up by focusing on sense
making.

00:27:19.515 --> 00:27:20.684
She asked them for the answer and

00:27:20.684 --> 00:27:23.594
then asks the students how to make sense
of their answer with a representation.

00:27:23.594 --> 00:27:25.658
She you ask them for the answer and

00:27:25.658 --> 00:27:30.617
then asks the students how to make sense
of their answer with a representation.

00:27:30.617 --> 00:27:32.223
So, what you get from that,

00:27:32.223 --> 00:27:37.041
is there are very, there were many
different ways of seeing the problem and

00:27:37.041 --> 00:27:41.217
students have different methods and
different representations.

00:27:41.217 --> 00:27:49.630
So the, many of the kids in the class got
the answer six.

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:53.969
You know, do so maybe, two and three comes
out with six, and, but

00:27:53.969 --> 00:27:57.490
then they really struggled to make sense
of that.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:59.332
So, Cathy uses this in the classroom.

00:27:59.332 --> 00:28:02.978
I really like this and it's really good
for the common core.

00:28:02.978 --> 00:28:07.416
And it comes from a book by some English
authors actually,

00:28:07.416 --> 00:28:12.193
where they talk about telling students as
a three-stage process,

00:28:12.193 --> 00:28:16.915
really three levels or three standards of
a convincing argument.

00:28:16.915 --> 00:28:19.020
Now, kids need to be making convincing
arguments with the new your common core.

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:24.930
And what they say, is that the lowest
standard is when you convince yourself.

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:29.270
We're usually pretty easy to convince
ourselves.

00:28:29.270 --> 00:28:31.040
The next level up is when you convince a
friend, and

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.520
the third level up is when you convince a
skeptic.

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:37.140
Actually, in the book they say convince an
enemy,

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:39.919
and Kathy changed it to a skeptic, I
think.

00:28:39.919 --> 00:28:43.402
anyway, she has asked the kids to be
skeptics, and

00:28:43.402 --> 00:28:47.869
I did this, actually just the past week
with a group of teachers.

00:28:47.869 --> 00:28:48.514
It was great.

00:28:48.514 --> 00:28:50.879
And I said okay, really got to be
skeptics.

00:28:50.879 --> 00:28:53.565
You've got, got to push any answer you
get,

00:28:53.565 --> 00:28:57.184
any reasoning to make sure it completely
makes sense to you.

00:28:57.184 --> 00:28:58.044
Be very skeptical.

00:28:58.044 --> 00:28:58.782
How do you know that?

00:28:58.782 --> 00:29:01.090
How do we, kids love being a skeptic.

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:03.420
You can get them all to be skeptics with
each other and

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.110
they take on that role really readily.

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:13.970
But it's also really helpful in pushing
other students to convince and

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:17.070
reason, so very mathematical things, too.

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:20.840
So, I wanted to tell you about a wonderful
task that helps kids be more convincing.

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:25.960
And in fact, I used this task just last
week with the role of a skeptic,

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:29.050
where some people had to convince others
and the others were skeptics.

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:34.690
So, this isn't the whole task, but the
whole task is on YouCubed and

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:37.250
it comes from Mark Driscoll, and

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:41.820
it's a book called Fostering Geometric
Thinking, and it's a paper folding task.

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:46.051
This is one of my favorites, and I think
this is great for lots of age levels.

00:29:46.051 --> 00:29:49.154
So they, I'm only showing you a little
extract of the whole task, but

00:29:49.154 --> 00:29:51.887
they start with a square piece of paper,
and they have to do all

00:29:51.887 --> 00:29:54.860
these different constructions, and just
one of them's up here.

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:57.396
Construct another square.

00:29:57.396 --> 00:30:00.973
With half of the area that is oriented
differently than the one you

00:30:00.973 --> 00:30:03.920
constructed in four, with the whole list
question.

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:05.130
Explain how you know it has half of the
area.

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:06.381
So this is a great task.

00:30:06.381 --> 00:30:09.881
Kids actually have to make this, and fold
their square, and

00:30:09.881 --> 00:30:13.996
then they have to be really convincing
that they know that this is a.

00:30:13.996 --> 00:30:16.460
Square so what you see as they do this.

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:18.740
They push to be very precise to be very
mathematical.

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:20.540
To use the right words.

00:30:20.540 --> 00:30:24.970
Particularly if you have other people
being skeptics.

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:28.490
So I love that task on YouTube there's a
high school unit.

00:30:28.490 --> 00:30:31.250
So theres there units on YouTube at the
moment.

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:34.880
One for elementary on for middle on for
high.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:37.360
The high school one, actually is one we
wrote at Stanford, and

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:39.090
it's over 100 pages of great activities.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:43.050
And this paper folding activity is one of
them, it's on page 87.

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:46.950
So if you wanna go to that task you can
buy

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:52.920
the book from Heinemann in the links
there, and it's, it's a great book.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:55.140
Or you can get the task in that way.

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:58.143
So an example of something that I want to

00:30:58.143 --> 00:31:01.971
share with you now comes from a five week
algebra class.

00:31:01.971 --> 00:31:06.960
That I actually, taught with graduate
students in summer school.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:08.480
So it was a summer school, seven and

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:10.280
eighth grade kids, who were mainly under
achieving.

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:11.060
And we, our goal

00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:15.680
in teaching the class was to teach algebra
as a problem solving tool.

00:31:15.680 --> 00:31:19.820
And we drew from different places,
Rathore, Ruth Parker, Mark Driscoll.

00:31:19.820 --> 00:31:23.620
Some English schemes, but one of the
things we did was we kept

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:27.780
all of the tasks open and we actually got
amazing participation from the kids.

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:30.968
My book, What's Math Got To Do With It?

00:31:30.968 --> 00:31:35.170
Describes what happened in the summer
school in more detail in the tasks.

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:37.800
But we basically gave them a lot of
algebraic pattern tasks.

00:31:37.800 --> 00:31:40.960
They hadn't learned algebra and they
learned algebra through that and

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:42.770
did really well in algebra afterwards.

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:44.860
But, and we kept the task open.

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:47.800
So I wanted to share with one strength.

00:31:47.800 --> 00:31:53.630
One issue that teachers have is kids
always at work at different levels.

00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:55.890
They are different achievement levels.

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:57.020
They work at different speeds.

00:31:57.020 --> 00:31:59.390
One thing I like to do in my own teaching
with kids.

00:31:59.390 --> 00:32:01.220
Is always to have a strategy up my sleeve.

00:32:01.220 --> 00:32:06.160
Which is to get any kids who finish to
either extend the tasks their given or or

00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:08.070
ask a new question.

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:12.810
So like this has happened a lot in summer
school and

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:15.970
this is one example we gave the kids.

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:18.620
This task right and there are two ways to
get this task.

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:21.640
Find the total number of blocks in a
hundred block high stair,

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:24.650
staircase is a kind of traditional way of
asking that question.

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:26.560
The way I like to ask it and

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:30.980
you may have seen before, is asking people
how the shape grows visually.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:34.800
And from there they get to the hundred
block question,

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:37.940
but much more conceptually.

00:32:37.940 --> 00:32:38.820
But anyway.

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:42.960
We asked when kids finished we just said
okay you now over to you you come up

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:45.230
with your own question.

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.300
One of the boys who got all F's in math
class came up with this lovely extension.

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:52.791
Which is much harder conceptually at where
they were looking at staircases and

00:32:52.791 --> 00:32:55.970
all of the kids started doing this so.

00:32:55.970 --> 00:33:01.730
My strategy really around this is whenever
you're working with short math questions,

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:03.130
with long math questions.

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:05.960
Great strategy with kids is when they get
to the end just say to them: now

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:06.480
over to you.

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:09.890
You write the question and try and make it
a hard question.

00:33:09.890 --> 00:33:13.610
And then have kids give their questions to
each other.

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:16.140
It involves kids in a much deeper way.

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:19.110
It helps them with the mathematics.

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:22.040
It helps them in all sorts of ways, so I
really like that strategy.

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:24.860
But so, generally we know that when maths
tasks are more open,

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:27.500
opened up for different ways, seeing,
different pathways.

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:30.560
Different representations, the
opportunities for learning and

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:33.070
for developing a growth mindset are
increased.

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:37.490
So a couple other things to share just
before we finish.

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:40.570
Not gonna say much about speed,

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:43.630
other than we do know that math should not
be associated with speed.

00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:47.770
And the article you see there just came
out in NCTM's journal, and

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:52.380
it's also downloadable on youcubed, about
the damage caused by timed tests.

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:54.240
That is the dimension of that.

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:57.535
But what I wanted to finish on the last
couple of strategies is I shared in,

00:33:57.535 --> 00:33:58.558
in the teacher class.

00:33:58.558 --> 00:34:03.670
And we spend a bit of time discussing how
I teach these classes for

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:06.416
teaching math for a growth mindset.

00:34:06.416 --> 00:34:08.425
And Carol Dweck came into the first one
and

00:34:08.425 --> 00:34:11.023
said something which shocked all the math
teachers.

00:34:11.023 --> 00:34:17.570
Which was every time a student makes a
mistake in math a synapse fires.

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:21.110
And actually we have new research evidence
just from the past year that

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:24.960
shows that when kids make a mistake in
math, brain growth occurs.

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:27.820
Fire, electric stimulants fire in their
brain,

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:31.510
But when they get work correct, no brain
growth happens.

00:34:32.690 --> 00:34:36.700
And they don't have to go back and correct
the mistake which a lot of people think,

00:34:36.700 --> 00:34:41.320
it's really the point of struggle that
causes that brain growth.

00:34:41.320 --> 00:34:43.940
So this has huge implications for us I
think as math teachers.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:47.550
It means that we really want students
making mistakes.

00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:51.560
Mistakes, or when their brains are growing
the most.

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:54.060
But they need to feel safe in the
environment to do that and

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:56.450
know that mistakes are valued.

00:34:56.450 --> 00:35:00.220
Really great for teachers just to start
the year saying I love mistakes.

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:03.170
Mistakes are when you're brains grow.

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:04.830
I want to see mistakes,

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:08.510
you'd be amazed how much that puts kids at
ease mathematically.

00:35:10.070 --> 00:35:10.970
So here's my source strategy.

00:35:10.970 --> 00:35:14.490
This actually came from a teacher in the
online class so in the mistake session.

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:17.510
I ask people to design a sort of new way
of handling mistakes in the classroom.

00:35:17.510 --> 00:35:20.270
And I'd love to hear who the teacher was
who suggested this.

00:35:20.270 --> 00:35:24.870
So if you're listening, feel free to email
me.

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.460
But one of the teachers in the class
submitted her idea.

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:28.500
And she said this.

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:32.808
She said at the start of classes now she
gets kids to crumple up a piece of paper.

00:35:32.808 --> 00:35:36.479
And throw it at the board with the
feelings they have when they make

00:35:36.479 --> 00:35:37.920
a mistake.

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:41.230
And then she gets them to unfold the
paper.

00:35:41.230 --> 00:35:42.360
And draw a little brain on it.

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:45.810
And then just draw over and color all the
lines from the crumpled up paper.

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:49.890
And that represents all the synaptic
firing of making mistake.

00:35:49.890 --> 00:35:52.600
And she, she gets kids to keep that to
remind them.

00:35:52.600 --> 00:36:00.150
Another thing I loved comes from one of
the teachers in Vista, Kim Hallowell.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:05.130
Who took from the course all the messages
that came from the course,

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:06.850
about the value of mistakes and other
things.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:10.610
And she asked kids to, to design a picture
of their own brains.

00:36:10.610 --> 00:36:13.450
And you can see there all, they were

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:18.590
told about the parts of the brain that
sort of see in black and white and color.

00:36:18.590 --> 00:36:22.810
And so they each designed their own brain,
and they put their favorite message on it.

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:24.230
Cause it's a beautiful visual and

00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:27.890
you can see some of the different messages
they chose, the kids chose.

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:32.460
Easy is a waste of time, I don't know this
yet,

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:35.510
embrace the struggle, I can grow my math
brain.

